195
00:16:30,150 --> 00:16:38,949
Can you tell us some of the details about what specific code it's actually generating and how it handles things like templating or other issues of that nature?

196
00:16:38,959 --> 00:16:44,790
Yeah, so with Swagger codegen, you've got your API spec as the input file.

197
00:16:44,790 --> 00:16:54,790
So that's going to go into the generator and what you're going to tell the generator as part of the parameters is what is the language that you're building for?

198
00:16:54,790 --> 00:16:56,470
What's the output type that you're looking for?

199
00:16:56,470 --> 00:16:59,351
So, is this a server stub? Is this a client SDK?

200
00:16:59,351 --> 00:17:03,590
Or it'll even put documentation out as well.

201
00:17:03,590 --> 00:17:09,270
After you've fed it into the generator, next it's going to go to the parser, which needs to dreference the API.

202
00:17:09,270 --> 00:17:11,350
So, you've probably seen a lot of APIs have refs in them.

203
00:17:11,350 --> 00:17:15,110
So, they're referencing another part of the spec or schema.

204
00:17:15,110 --> 00:17:16,870
Could even be something outside the spec.

205
00:17:16,870 --> 00:17:18,549
So, like a publicly available thing.

206
00:17:18,549 --> 00:17:21,189
In order to generate code, you have to take that reference and fill it in with the actual content.

207
00:17:21,189 --> 00:17:33,750
So the parser is going to do that for you, flatten out the spec, and then it'll break it down into components and into a map, feed that into a language specific generator based on what you've passed in in your parameters.

208
00:17:33,750 --> 00:17:38,230
And then it's going to go to a templating engine, so handlebars or mustache.

209
00:17:38,230 --> 00:17:42,390
That templating engine is then going to build the code in the format that you like.

210
00:17:42,390 --> 00:17:49,270
So people have particular coding standards or code bases or you specifically every time you run codegen, you want that same format.

211
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:51,990
You want the code to come out the same. You want it like repeatable.

212
00:17:51,990 --> 00:17:55,830
You don't want your downstream code to be impacted just because you regenerated the code.

213
00:17:55,840 --> 00:18:01,430
So that template engine is what's going to help you get it to your format in the same way every time.

214
00:18:01,430 --> 00:18:03,029
And then the output of that will be a bunch of build files.

215
00:18:03,039 --> 00:18:04,870
Those get bundled together.

216
00:18:04,870 --> 00:18:07,110
Then you've got your server stub or your client SDK.

217
00:18:07,120 --> 00:18:08,710
Okay, that's really helpful.

218
00:18:08,710 --> 00:18:12,630
Now you mentioned things like how handlebars could be your templating engine.

219
00:18:12,630 --> 00:18:14,470
to really land this point.

220
00:18:14,470 --> 00:18:20,789
Can you say a little bit more about why templating engines are so powerful at this stage of building an API?

221
00:18:20,799 --> 00:18:29,830
I think people have very particular coding standards or styles or just the way they want their backend to be set up.

222
00:18:29,830 --> 00:18:32,789
And so if we as Smart Bear just said, well, this is how we generate code.

223
00:18:32,789 --> 00:18:37,669
Well, that's just not going to work for a whole lot of people and companies and they're going to have to do manual processing afterwards.

224
00:18:37,669 --> 00:18:41,830
And a lot of people run Swagger code gen every build.

225
00:18:41,830 --> 00:18:49,270
So you've got a spec and even if the spec is the same or it was updated, they're running it through codegen, regenerating their server subs and their client SDKs,

226
00:18:49,270 --> 00:18:54,549
if they had to go and manually make changes to how that code was formatted or or how it was set up every time.

227
00:18:54,549 --> 00:18:57,590
And that really kind of takes away the usefulness of codegen to begin with.

228
00:18:57,590 --> 00:19:03,190
So that's where those templating engines come in super handy is that you can tailor it to what you need and how you want that output to be.

229
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:14,789
All right, that makes a lot of sense. Now when I was learning more about some of the open- source projects that SmartWayware helps to maintain, I remember reading about Spectral and then there was another tool called Prism.

230
00:19:14,789 --> 00:19:23,270
Can you tell us a little bit about those tools and then how they play into the overall creation or testing of a Swagger API?

231
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:25,909
Yeah, absolutely.

232
00:19:25,909 --> 00:19:31,590
So Spectral is a linting governance standardization tool that you will run against your API.

233
00:19:31,590 --> 00:19:35,669
So there's some rules you can get out of the box if you're using Swagger Studio.

234
00:19:35,679 --> 00:19:40,710
So this comes with a whole bunch of rules and standards, but you can add your own custom spectral rules.

235
00:19:40,710 --> 00:19:46,150
And basically what you're saying is these are the behaviors or the styles in my API that I want.

236
00:19:46,150 --> 00:19:47,669
There's some nice public style guides available.

237
00:19:47,669 --> 00:19:54,070
So there's like an OASP top 10 public style guide that has a whole bunch of spectral rules built into it.

238
00:19:54,070 --> 00:19:57,029
So you get a little security from your spec.

239
00:19:57,029 --> 00:20:05,510
And then say you don't want any verbs in endpoint names or you want things kebab case or camel case or there's so much that you can do with spectral which

240
00:20:05,510 --> 00:20:14,310
when you're an organization and you have an API program you generally don't want all of your teams just doing their own thing you want to set a standard for the company for the organization.

241
00:20:14,310 --> 00:20:24,950
So spectral comes in super handy that is an open source project but it's heavily built into swagger studio in terms of like how we're doing standardization governance.

242
00:20:24,950 --> 00:20:27,350
So yeah, that one's directly embedded into Swagger Studio.

243
00:20:27,350 --> 00:20:30,230
So Prism, that's an API mocking library.

244
00:20:30,230 --> 00:20:31,750
So you would have developed your spec first.

245
00:20:31,760 --> 00:20:41,669
You have your spec and say the backend team hasn't built the API yet, but your testers or your front end or someone wants to start playing around with the API, seeing how it's going to behave.

246
00:20:41,669 --> 00:20:43,669
So Prism is a mocking tool.

247
00:20:43,669 --> 00:20:47,270
It'll take the spec, it'll serve something up that people can interact with.

248
00:20:47,270 --> 00:20:52,230
Now that's not directly implemented in Swagger Studio, but that's cuz we already have a fullyfledged mocking tool.

249
00:20:52,230 --> 00:20:55,190
hasn't made sense to replace it, but something we already had that existed.

250
00:20:55,200 --> 00:21:01,270
Okay, so in summary, Spectral is largely for API linting.

251
00:21:01,270 --> 00:21:06,870
Prism is largely for API mocking, and I can use those as a part of the overall Swagger ecosystem.

252
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:08,549
Did I catch that correctly?

253
00:21:08,559 --> 00:21:11,510
Exactly. Okay, good.

254
00:21:11,510 --> 00:21:21,590
Now, one of the things that I wanted to dive into next was connected to like the various workflows and integration practices when it comes to using Swagger.

255
00:21:21,590 --> 00:21:33,029
And later in the episode, we're going to talk even further about how you could use AI to build an API or actually how an AI coding agent could consume an API.

256
00:21:33,029 --> 00:21:43,430
But before we get to that, my sense is that there's a tension because as we've been talking, we've been talking about like making the spec or making the contract first.

257
00:21:43,430 --> 00:21:53,990
And I know that there are also ways where I can just simply dive in and implement the API directly and then I don't have to worry about codegen or various tools of that nature.

258
00:21:53,990 --> 00:21:59,029
So can you give our listeners a good lay of the land and talk about the tradeoff between these approaches?

259
00:21:59,039 --> 00:22:07,990
So throughout my career I've worked with a whole bunch of teams, a bunch of different companies, different projects with different number of consumers and honestly we've done it both ways.

260
00:22:07,990 --> 00:22:09,750
I've worked on code first teams.

261
00:22:09,750 --> 00:22:12,549
I've worked on API design first teams.

262
00:22:12,549 --> 00:22:18,070
You could be successful with either one, but there are definitely pros and cons to either approach that you want to take.

263
00:22:18,070 --> 00:22:28,149
I guess what I would say is historically the biggest objection to API design first would be the amount of time that you feel that you're spending defining that spec up front.

264
00:22:28,149 --> 00:22:36,470
And so I'm thinking back like 5 10 years going into Swagger editor as a blank slate and typing out a bunch of YAML a bunch of JSON and

265
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:49,270
learning about open API different versions of the specs could kind of know feel like a daunting task to take on and it could take a while to get done and I could see why teams might not want to do that.

266
00:22:49,270 --> 00:22:58,710
What I would say though is with the tooling available today and especially a lot of like we spend a lot of time in studio is taking away those barriers and just making it really fast and easy to build that API up front.

267
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:05,909
And so I mean I generally would highly advocate for design first and there's a couple reasons for that.

268
00:23:05,909 --> 00:23:08,070
So one is just unlocking parallel development.

269
00:23:08,070 --> 00:23:18,950
So, I love that analogy of building a bridge from both sides and you've got that contract in the middle and that contract is what's going to ensure that they're connecting in the right spot rather than just hoping that they do.

270
00:23:18,950 --> 00:23:25,430
But even worse than that is only one side can start and the other side other team's just waiting and waiting and waiting until it's done.

271
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:30,470
So, I had a project late last year where one team was building an API.

272
00:23:30,470 --> 00:23:32,149
We did design first.

273
00:23:32,149 --> 00:23:34,149
They all agreed on the contract and the spec.

274
00:23:34,149 --> 00:23:36,950
The consuming team built out their service.

275
00:23:36,950 --> 00:23:42,950
They actually had it pushed out in production and live ready to go before the providing service was even done.

276
00:23:42,950 --> 00:23:45,110
They moved on to other things.

277
00:23:45,110 --> 00:23:51,190
And then when the providing service was done, they pushed it to production and it seamlessly all went and and everything was working.

278
00:23:51,190 --> 00:23:57,830
Otherwise, they would have been waiting that whole time for that first team to be done to have the code written to then be able to build on top of it.

279
00:23:57,830 --> 00:24:03,990
And so it really can be an accelerator in terms of teams if you've got distributed teams that are dependent on each other.

280
00:24:03,990 --> 00:24:13,510
The other big thing with design first is that when you have organizational standards, I really think that it's very healthy for an organization API program,

281
00:24:13,510 --> 00:24:17,190
define those standards, define your governance rules, define your models and your domains.

282
00:24:17,200 --> 00:24:18,870
What should your data look like?

283
00:24:18,870 --> 00:24:22,390
Don't let each team decide what to call a field.

284
00:24:22,390 --> 00:24:28,870
You can end up with like really a mess across your whole APIs if there isn't some sort of governance or oversight on them.

285
00:24:28,870 --> 00:24:34,710
When you do design first, you can catch most of those things right away before the specs even before a line of code is written.

286
00:24:34,710 --> 00:24:40,390
Really worst case is the code's written deployed, you catch it later.

287
00:24:40,390 --> 00:24:45,590
And if you have to version that API just to make a change and now support two versions, that's a horrible experience for a team.

288
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:48,789
So catching those errors up front can save you a lot of time.

289
00:24:48,789 --> 00:24:55,590
And then when you look at something like codegen, if you write your spec up front, it can generate your server subs, your client SDKs.

290
00:24:55,590 --> 00:24:58,230
So I don't even know that you're really losing any time by following that practice.

291
00:24:58,240 --> 00:24:59,910
Thanks. That was really helpful.

292
00:24:59,910 --> 00:25:09,590
So you mentioned the bridge a moment ago and you also talked about the idea of a contract and that made me think of something that's called birectional contract testing.

293
00:25:09,590 --> 00:25:12,950
Can you help our listeners to understand what that process actually looks like?

294
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:14,710
Yeah.

295
00:25:14,710 --> 00:25:16,710
So people may be familiar with Packflow.

296
00:25:16,710 --> 00:25:18,630
So now Swagger contract testing is the name of the product.

297
00:25:18,630 --> 00:25:28,710
But birectional contract testing is an amazing feature that the team has built where historically API functional testing is your QA,

298
00:25:28,710 --> 00:25:40,630
your testing team looking at your own API that your team built, testing out the functionality, happy path, sad path, they're trying to catch everything, but there's really no focus on how people are actually using your API.

299
00:25:40,630 --> 00:25:43,430
Like what are your consumers doing?

300
00:25:43,430 --> 00:25:47,990
So birectional contract testing is built around the premise of the provider says what it does.

301
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:58,630
All of your consumers say how they're using it and then anytime you make a change, you can see if that change is actually going to break that relationship between the provider and the consumer.

302
00:25:58,630 --> 00:26:04,070
And so I'm sure many people have been in a situation where you would need to make a quick bug fix.

303
00:26:04,070 --> 00:26:08,070
It does affect the API, but you really don't want to version the whole API just to make this change.

304
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:19,830
Well, birectional contract testing is going to catch like, oh yeah, that's a totally safe change to make because none of your consumers are using that field or here's the two out of the five consumers that are using that field.

305
00:26:19,830 --> 00:26:29,590
You two can coordinate together and work on and manage like a coordinated release as opposed to just having no idea who uses it and therefore you have no choice but to version the API.

306
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:32,470
Aha, I got it.

307
00:26:32,470 --> 00:26:37,669
So often times when we think about testing and we've already talked about linting and how there's a tool that helps with linting,

308
00:26:37,669 --> 00:26:46,789
we think about running those types of things like in a CI/CD portfolio, maybe on GitHub actions or another system of that nature.

309
00:26:46,789 --> 00:26:53,830
If I'm building Swagger APIs, are there ways that I can run these testing techniques and linting techniques in some kind of CI/CD pipeline?

310
00:26:53,840 --> 00:26:56,470
Yeah, absolutely.

311
00:26:56,470 --> 00:26:58,070
So say you got your spec first.

312
00:26:58,070 --> 00:27:00,390
So one of the things you can do is run the Swagger CLI.

313
00:27:00,390 --> 00:27:03,750
that's going to make sure that your spec is just a valid spec.

314
00:27:03,750 --> 00:27:06,790
I mean, that's kind of like step zero. Is the spec actually valid?

315
00:27:06,799 --> 00:27:13,990
So, the contract test product has a feature that I absolutely love, which is called provider drift.

316
00:27:13,990 --> 00:27:19,909
So, what that's going to do is in your build pipeline, it's going to look at that spec, it's going to look at your running code.

317
00:27:19,909 --> 00:27:23,990
It's going to compare the two and say, does your running code match your spec?

318
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:28,470
And so, if those two don't match, we have a problem.

319
00:27:28,470 --> 00:27:39,669
So either your spec is out of date, which means your documentation's going to be out of date, or your code is wrong because they didn't implement the spec correctly, and that needs to be addressed so that those two things are in sync.

320
00:27:39,669 --> 00:27:44,070
And so that's just a build process pipeline check to say, hey, do are these two things in line?

321
00:27:44,070 --> 00:27:45,830
Are they doing what they're supposed to do?

322
00:27:45,830 --> 00:27:47,430
So really, it's a nice way to catch that.

323
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:55,909
And then say everything's good, your code matches your spec, it's a valid spec, you can use tools like the Swagger Studio CLI or the registry API.

324
00:27:55,909 --> 00:27:57,909
And so you can publish that spec.

325
00:27:57,909 --> 00:28:06,870
So say you wanted to make it publicly available to your external consumers or you could use that as a private repository for your internal customers to use as well.

326
00:28:06,870 --> 00:28:14,710
So you have kind of that visibility of is it public, is it private, but still that can be the central repo for where people are going to see documentation.

327
00:28:14,710 --> 00:28:21,990
And then the portal product is where if you're building more like advanced documentation or workflows, you can update the spec there.

328
00:28:21,990 --> 00:28:24,070
So now the latest copy of the spec is available.

329
00:28:24,070 --> 00:28:28,710
Okay, that gives me a good sense on how I can use swagger tools in a CI/CD pipeline.

330
00:28:28,710 --> 00:28:33,110
I remember a while ago you talked about packed or packed flow.

331
00:28:33,110 --> 00:28:35,350
Can you tell us a little bit more about what that tool is?

332
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:47,590
I think kind of covered in two different ways, but so birectional contract testing and provider drift sort of the two main functions of packlow and those are built on the open source project pacted which is available.

333
00:28:47,590 --> 00:28:57,190
So that's another like open core model where pact is available and then that's embedded within pactflow as the the commercial product you know it's been renamed swagger contract testing.

334
00:28:57,200 --> 00:28:59,110
Okay that was really helpful.

335
00:28:59,110 --> 00:29:11,669
So the open- source tool is called pacted and then your commercial offering that goes along with that is called packflow and those tools actually provide what we call birectional contract testing. Is that the right way to say it?

336
00:29:11,679 --> 00:29:13,750
Yep.

337
00:29:13,750 --> 00:29:22,470
Birectional contract testing and provider drift would be in the swagger contract test product and then pacted supports consumerdriven contract testing.

338
00:29:22,470 --> 00:29:24,390
Thanks for covering those details. That was really helpful.

339
00:29:24,390 --> 00:29:26,389
Now, one of the things I know is that

340
00:29:26,389 --> 00:29:38,310
the swagger ecosystem has mechanisms in place to help you to use AI in order to actually generate the API or to test components of the API.

341
00:29:38,310 --> 00:29:40,310
So, I'd like to start there first.

342
00:29:40,310 --> 00:29:49,430
Can you tell us a little bit about how AI supports the process of designing the API all the way to documenting the API? What else should we learn about here?

343
00:29:49,440 --> 00:29:51,590
Yeah, absolutely.

344
00:29:51,590 --> 00:29:56,870
So, we've invested pretty heavily in AI over the past year because I mean obviously that's that's the way the world is going,

345
00:29:56,870 --> 00:30:09,269
but I mentioned earlier that someone may have spent hours, days, weeks typing out an API spec in that code editor, and that's just not where anyone wants to be spending their time.

346
00:30:09,269 --> 00:30:17,350
So one of the easiest things that we've done is create a natural language form for creating an API from scratch.

347
00:30:17,350 --> 00:30:23,669
And so say a engineer is working with their product manager to define the requirements the API what it should do.

348
00:30:23,669 --> 00:30:28,870
They can then take that paste that into Swagger Studio and then it's going to go and generate your spec.

349
00:30:28,870 --> 00:30:38,470
But not only is it generating your spec, it's going to do it following your standardization and governance rules that you've created and it's going to conform to that out of the box.

350
00:30:38,470 --> 00:30:49,110
So right away you're going to get an API that comes back that's you know maybe it's not 100% perfect but 85 90% of that work is all done for you and now you just kind of need to review it.

351
00:30:49,110 --> 00:30:57,029
And one of the nice things is it's it's not just generate from scratch but if you want to make updates you can use AI to do that as well.

352
00:30:57,029 --> 00:31:06,070
So you could say for example you missed 500 responses add 500 responses everywhere or you know add a 401 here and that'll just go do it for you.

353
00:31:06,070 --> 00:31:14,549
And so the amount of time you're spending writing JSON and YAML specific to Open API 3.1 for example just gets smaller and smaller over time.

354
00:31:14,559 --> 00:31:26,789
So are there specific large language models or cloud providers that you recommend or that you commonly use when it comes to this approach for AI enabled API generation?

355
00:31:26,789 --> 00:31:29,590
So we actually have a team.

356
00:31:29,590 --> 00:31:36,230
Their job is to go through and check with the latest models and which ones are working best and which ones work for each tool.

357
00:31:36,230 --> 00:31:40,149
So there's not necessarily like a blanket answer of which model, which tools working best.

358
00:31:40,159 --> 00:31:46,950
So we're looking at, you know, anthropic models, Gemini models, open AAI models, and so all of them just whichever one.

359
00:31:46,960 --> 00:31:50,310
And sometimes the latest one is, you know, not necessarily the best.

360
00:31:50,320 --> 00:31:56,470
Sometimes it is, but we're always trying to balance what's the right one for this function that that's being developed now.

361
00:31:56,480 --> 00:31:59,430
Okay.

362
00:31:59,430 --> 00:32:06,950
So, if I'm actually using AI to generate my API or I think I can use API, I'm sorry, AI and actually to generate the test cases,

363
00:32:06,950 --> 00:32:13,350
do I get to pick the model that I want to use as an LLM or is that something that the tool suite picks for me?

364
00:32:13,360 --> 00:32:15,110
Yeah.

365
00:32:15,110 --> 00:32:21,909
So in so if you're doing it through our tool which again I would recommend cuz it's going to use all your standardization governance rules schemas templates that type of thing.

366
00:32:21,909 --> 00:32:28,549
We're picking the model so you don't need to worry about it but you also don't need to worry about the cost associated with it cuz that we're just dealing with that.

367
00:32:28,559 --> 00:32:30,310
Okay that's helpful.

368
00:32:30,310 --> 00:32:39,830
Now one of the things in the context of AI is something that's called an MCP server and in a way I think this is connected to API creation and documentation.

369
00:32:39,830 --> 00:32:46,870
So can you tell us a little bit what is an MCP server and how does that connect to the AI and API landscape?

370
00:32:46,880 --> 00:33:00,149
Yeah, so I think it was late 2024 was Anthropic came out with the concept of MCP servers and this was a way to extend an LLM and give it access to information that it wouldn't normally have.

371
00:33:00,149 --> 00:33:07,430
And so say you know a model was trained in August of 2025 and that's the latest set of information it has.

372
00:33:07,430 --> 00:33:15,909
when you connect an MCP server to it, it now can access the tools and APIs that you've given it to in that MCP server.

373
00:33:15,909 --> 00:33:21,190
So for us, for example, we have an MCP server for Swagger.

374
00:33:21,190 --> 00:33:26,870
So if you hook that up to cloud code and say, give me a list of all the APIs that I have.

375
00:33:26,870 --> 00:33:33,669
It literally can see up to the minute the list of APIs that you have access to, it can pull them down into say cloud code.

376
00:33:33,669 --> 00:33:39,830
you can now start using them, iterating them, build code off of them.

377
00:33:39,830 --> 00:33:43,110
And in early 2025, this became a standard that just every vendor started implementing.

378
00:33:43,110 --> 00:33:44,950
So it wasn't just anthropic.

379
00:33:44,950 --> 00:33:47,110
It just it took off like crazy.

380
00:33:47,110 --> 00:33:56,310
